Q: Use of Static Keyword with Example:
In Java, the static keyword is used to define members (variables, methods, blocks, and nested classes) that belong to the class itself rather than to any specific object. 
This means they are shared across all instances and memory is allocated only once when the class is loaded.

Key points:
Access static members using the class name without creating an object.
Static members cannot directly access non-static members.
Static methods cannot use this or super.

Example:
package statickeyword_example;
class Student {
    String name;
    static String School;

}


public class StaticKeyword_Example {

    
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student.School="ABC";
        Student Student1=new Student();
        Student1.name="Tony";
        System.out.println(Student1.School);
        
        Student Student2=new Student();
        Student2.name="Rahul";
        System.out.println(Student2.School);
    }
    
}

OUTPUT:
ABC
ABC

